---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/tested-on.json';

const report = 'https://github.com/UrDeck/urdeck/issues/new?template=hardware-report.yml';
---
<Base title="Tested on" description="The displays and machines UrDeck has been run on, what is expected to work, and how to send a report.">
  <h1>Tested on</h1>
  <p>
    UrDeck was made for one panel and is checked on the hardware listed here. This list starts with one entry; it grows
    with reports.
  </p>

  <h2>Tested</h2>
  {data.entries.map((e) => (
    <section>
      <h3>{e.display}</h3>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Connection</th><td>{e.connection}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Resolution</th><td>{e.resolution}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">GPU</th><td>{e.gpu}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Windows</th><td>{e.windows}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>What was checked:</p>
      <ul>{e.checked.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}</ul>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>Expected, not tested</h2>
  <p>
    A display that Windows treats as a monitor is expected to work: UrDeck lays out a grid of square cells from the
    monitor's width, so the size and orientation should not matter. That is an expectation, not a result, wherever it is
    not listed above. GPU load and temperature come from Windows and should work with any GPU; power and clock readings
    need NVIDIA's driver library today and show a dash on AMD and Intel.
  </p>

  <h2>Does not work</h2>
  <p>
    Small USB LCD screens that Windows does not treat as a monitor (those driven by their own software through a
    serial or HID protocol) do not work: UrDeck draws on a display Windows knows about.
  </p>

  <h2>Send a report</h2>
  <p>
    If you ran UrDeck on something, tell me how it went, whether it worked or not.
    <a href={report}>Open the hardware report form</a>. It asks for the display and how it is connected, your GPU, your
    Windows version, what worked, and the lines <code>urdeck.log</code> writes at startup (the monitors, the display
    chosen, the background surface and the source of each reading). Look through those lines before you post them.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    HYTE and HYTE Y70 Touch are trademarks of their owner, mentioned here only to say what UrDeck was tested on. UrDeck is
    not affiliated with HYTE. NVIDIA, GeForce and Windows are trademarks of their respective owners.
  </p>
</Base>
